# No rustc/cargo build-dependency is declared on purpose. tpmd declares
# rust-version = "1.94" and uses edition 2024, which is newer than the rustc of
# most currently supported Debian-like releases (only sid and trixie-backports
# reach it), so the build dependency could not be satisfied on most targets. The toolchain is supplied by the build environment
# instead; see debian/rules and docs/install.md for what that means and what
# would have to change for a package built in the archive.

Package: tpmd
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
# The backends above the guaranteed floor are separate packages, and a plain
# install is meant to have the whole ladder. Recommends, rather than Depends,
# because the daemon works without any of them — it falls back to `file` — and
# because the minimal install is a configuration someone may deliberately want:
# `apt-get install --no-install-recommends tpmd` gives exactly that.
Recommends: tpmd-backend-keyring, tpmd-backend-linux-tpm
Description: local key agent that signs on behalf of local applications
 tpmd owns private signing keys and signs on behalf of local applications over
 a local socket, so that a consumer never sees key material: it asks for a
 signature and gets one, or gets a refusal that says why.
 .
 Keys live at a security level — file, keyring, sealed or hardware — and the
 daemon selects the strongest level the machine can actually reach. It refuses
 to serve anything below the floor it was started with rather than quietly
 handing out a weaker key, it never re-keys a device to satisfy a request, and
 it never replaces a lost key with a weaker one.
 .
 This package installs the system-wide agent: a socket-activated service that
 serves every local user and authorises each caller from kernel peer
 credentials. A per-user agent can also be run by hand; the toy consumer used
 for end-to-end checks is built from the source tree rather than shipped.
 .
 The backends above the guaranteed `file` level are installed separately, one
 package each (recommended above), and are loaded by the daemon from
 /usr/lib/tpmd/backends at run time. The `file` backend is built in and is the
 floor: a machine with nothing else installed still serves, at the level that
 promises least.

Package: tpmd-backend-keyring
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: operating system credential store backend for tpmd
 tpmd owns private signing keys and signs on behalf of local applications.
 .
 This package provides the `keyring` rung of tpmd's security ladder: the key
 scalar is kept in the operating system's own credential store (the Secret
 Service over the session bus on Linux), so a copy of it needs the user's
 password rather than merely the file's permissions. Signing still happens in
 the daemon; only the custody of the scalar is stronger than the built-in
 `file` level.
 .
 The backend is a plugin, loaded by tpmd from /usr/lib/tpmd/backends. It owns
 nothing but that file and works whatever the daemon's version, so installing
 or removing it never disturbs the agent's own files, and a host with no
 session bus simply does not offer this rung.

Package: tpmd-backend-linux-tpm
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: Linux TPM 2.0 backend for tpmd
 tpmd owns private signing keys and signs on behalf of local applications.
 .
 This package provides the `sealed` and `hardware` rungs of tpmd's security
 ladder, through a TPM 2.0 device: at `sealed` the key scalar is wrapped by the
 TPM and inert anywhere else, and at `hardware` the key never leaves the TPM at
 all, not even to sign.
 .
 The backend is a plugin, loaded by tpmd from /usr/lib/tpmd/backends. It owns
 nothing but that file and works whatever the daemon's version, so installing
 or removing it never disturbs the agent's own files, and a machine whose TPM
 cannot be opened simply does not offer these rungs. Access to the device is
 granted by the daemon's package (the tss user and the udev rule), not here.
